/* Maui personality rebuild chapters 21–30, distinct demigod voice and agency. */
(function(){'use strict';const lib=window.JASPER_FANFIC_DATA;if(!lib||!Array.isArray(lib.series))return;const edits={"the-shore-i-choose":{"maui-21":{"number":21,"title":"The Guest Who Stayed","content":"Greg arrived at Tideglass Cove with two broken folding chairs and an unfinished song.\n\nI knew the song was unfinished because he'd hummed the same four notes through the Starwake Door, across the square, and down the steps to the beach without finding a fifth.\n\nMaui heard him before I did.\n\n\"Is that music,\" he asked, \"or is someone being chased by a very polite goat?\"\n\nGreg looked offended for a second.\n\nThen he laughed.\n\n\"Little of both.\"\n\nHe set the chairs beside the workshop. One had a bent hinge. The other wobbled because a screw had stripped the wood beneath its bracket.\n\n\"These are for the rest bench by the sea-glass orchard,\" he explained. \"Pip said the older ones weren't comfortable for people waiting for the ferry.\"\n\nMaui lifted the wobbly chair.\n\n\"I can fix that. Well, I can lift it until the hinge becomes somebody else's problem. The lifting is definitely the impressive part.\"\n\nGreg reached for the other.\n\n\"Hey, I've got tools.\"\n\nMaui flexed his shoulders.\n\n\"I can carry both.\"\n\n\"Great, but can you fix both?\"\n\n\"I can do anything.\"\n\nI closed my eyes.\n\nHere we went.\n\nThey were both trying to be kind. That was the problem. Maui wanted to be the helpful hero; Greg wanted to be the man who showed up when a job needed doing. Neither had asked the person who'd requested the chairs what the job actually involved.\n\nPip arrived carrying a folded sketch.\n\n\"Good, you're here,\" they said.\n\n\"We've got it,\" Maui announced.\n\n\"Do you?\" Pip asked.\n\nGreg held up a screwdriver.\n\n\"Mostly.\"\n\nPip unfolded the sketch.\n\nThe bench needed two chairs with stable seats and backs at slightly different heights. One person using the sheltered garden found it difficult to stand from the low seat. Another wanted armrests they could grip. The chairs also had to fit on the path without blocking anyone using wheels or a handcart.\n\nNeither broken chair was the right size.\n\nMaui looked at the drawing.\n\nGreg looked at the chairs.\n\nI bit the inside of my cheek to stop smiling.\n\n\"Maybe we should ask the person who needs them,\" I said.\n\nPip nodded.\n\n\"That was my next sentence.\"\n\nWe found Ilya, the garden steward's assistant, checking the first sea-kale bed. They had been the one to request seating because they spent long stretches weeding and sometimes needed to rest before walking back uphill.\n\n\"These are too low,\" Ilya said when we showed the chairs.\n\nGreg examined the legs.\n\n\"I could add blocks.\"\n\n\"That might make them unstable,\" Ilya said. \"And the path is narrow here.\"\n\nMaui put the chairs down.\n\n\"What would help?\"\n\nIlya looked surprised.\n\n\"A bench with a firm seat and two armrests. Nothing fancy. I want to sit without sinking into it, and I want to be able to stand without somebody having to pull me up.\"\n\nMaui nodded.\n\n\"Then let's build that.\"\n\nPip smiled.\n\n\"Now we're talking.\"\n\nThe afternoon became a lesson in designing for the person who would use the thing, rather than the person who wanted the satisfaction of finishing it.\n\nTamsin provided measurements. Edda checked the turning space. Ilya tested a temporary model made from a crate and two spare boards. Greg sanded the edges. Maui held the supports while the screws went in.\n\nI watched them work, amused by how quickly the competition dissolved once there was an actual specification.\n\nThen Greg started humming again.\n\nFour notes. A pause. Four notes.\n\nMaui looked at him.\n\n\"What's that song about?\"\n\nGreg shrugged.\n\n\"Coming back to a place that's changed.\"\n\n\"Sounds sad.\"\n\n\"Some of it is.\"\n\n\"Make it heroic.\"\n\nGreg laughed.\n\n\"That's one option.\"\n\nMaui began proposing lyrics involving storms, monsters, and the triumph of returning across a thousand seas. Greg tried the rhythm out of politeness. It was terrible.\n\n\"Wait,\" Maui announced, \"I haven't reached the part where the monster begs the great Maui for mercy.\"\n\nGreg rested his chin on his guitar. \"Yeah, that's probably gonna be a different song.\"\n\n\"Different? It's an instant classic!\"\n\nMini Maui appeared on his chest and began conducting the imaginary orchestra. He stopped when Greg plucked a soft, uncertain chord and looked thoughtful.\n\n\"What's that sound?\" Maui asked.\n\n\"The one I keep trying to write.\"\n\n\"Sounds like a boat scraping into a harbor after a bad day.\" Maui's grin turned almost shy. \"Not the part where everybody cheers. The part where the people you were worried about are actually there.\"\n\nGreg played the chord again. \"That's... kinda what I meant.\"\n\nMaui sat up, delighted. \"Ha! Brilliant. I contributed! Put that in the credits.\"\n\n\"You contributed a description,\" I said.\n\n\"A legendary description.\"\n\nGreg laughed and kept the next chord for himself. Maui was already composing a speech about being a gifted musician, but for once he waited until Greg finished the measure before giving it.\n\nI told them so.\n\n\"Both of you are making the song about the loudest person in the room,\" I said.\n\nGreg looked at me.\n\n\"Who should it be about?\"\n\n\"I don't know. It's your song.\"\n\nMaui folded his arms.\n\n\"You said it was terrible.\"\n\n\"It was. I didn't say you have to let me write it.\"\n\nGreg smiled.\n\n\"Fair.\"\n\nThey sat beside the unfinished bench while Ilya tested the armrest. For a few minutes nobody spoke.\n\nThen Greg played the four notes again, softer.\n\nIlya paused.\n\n\"That sounds like the ferry bell,\" they said.\n\nGreg looked up.\n\n\"The what?\"\n\n\"The little bell Fen rings when the tide turns and the upper path is open. It's not exciting. It just tells people when to come back.\"\n\nGreg stared at the water.\n\nThen he played a fifth note.\n\nIt fit.\n\nMaui looked at Ilya.\n\n\"That's what the song needed?\"\n\n\"Maybe.\"\n\nGreg smiled.\n\n\"Maybe.\"\n\nWe finished the bench before sunset. Ilya sat on it, stood without help, and sat again. Edda checked the path clearance and approved the location. The old chairs went back to Starwake for salvageable parts instead of being placed where they would be inconvenient.\n\nPip wrote a maintenance note for the next month's inspection.\n\nMaui looked at the finished bench.\n\n\"No plaque?\"\n\n\"No plaque,\" I said.\n\n\"Not even a small one?\"\n\nPip handed him a pencil.\n\n\"You may write the inspection date underneath.\"\n\nHe did.\n\nGreg stood beside the bench with his guitar.\n\n\"Can I play the song here when it's done?\"\n\nIlya considered the question.\n\n\"As long as you ask before you turn the orchard into a concert.\"\n\n\"Deal.\"\n\nThe evening air was cool. The tide had come in enough to cover the old stones where the second bell had sounded. The temporary closure sign remained in place. Someone had drawn a tiny crab in the corner.\n\nMaui slipped an arm around my shoulders.\n\n\"Greg's not bad,\" he said.\n\n\"He's not.\"\n\n\"Bit slow with the monsters.\"\n\n\"Not every song needs one.\"\n\nMaui grinned.\n\n\"Are you sure?\"\n\nGreg joined us on the path and looked between us.\n\n\"You two have any plans for dinner?\"\n\n\"I was going to ask Jasper if they'd like to come home,\" Maui said.\n\nGreg looked at me rather than assuming an answer.\n\n\"I was going to ask if you'd be free tomorrow to hear the new chorus. No pressure.\"\n\nI appreciated the difference. The relationships in my life did not have to turn every shared evening into a contest or a secret. Maui knew Greg and I cared about each other. Greg knew Maui mattered to me. Neither of them had to become anything more to the other for that to be true.\n\n\"Tomorrow afternoon works,\" I said. \"Tonight I'm going home with Maui.\"\n\nGreg smiled.\n\n\"Great. I'll bring the fifth note.\"\n\nMaui looked at him.\n\n\"Make it a heroic one.\"\n\n\"Maybe a modestly heroic one.\"\n\nThey laughed.\n\nOn the way home, Maui was quiet for a while.\n\n\"Did I make that awkward?\" he asked.\n\n\"A little.\"\n\nHe winced.\n\n\"You were trying to help. So was Greg. Ilya needed both of you to listen first.\"\n\nMaui thought about the bench.\n\n\"I think I'm getting better.\"\n\n\"You are.\"\n\nHe squeezed my hand.\n\n\"Does that make me less impressive?\"\n\nI stopped, turned toward him, and kissed his cheek.\n\n\"No. It makes you easier to love.\"\n\nHe blushed.\n\nThe great Maui, who had bragged about lassoing the sun, blushed beside a public bench.\n\nI decided not to mention it.\n\nAt home, he carved a second tiny fish into the broom handle. This one looked suspiciously like Greg's hair.\n\nI made him promise not to give it to Greg without asking.\n\nHe promised.\n\nMostly.","word_count":1420},"maui-22":{"number":22,"title":"Tea, Salt and Smoke","content":"Iroh arrived at Tideglass Cove carrying three teacups, a brass timer, and enough patience to make me suspicious.\n\nMaui was beside the new sea-glass orchard trying to light a cooking fire with wood he had collected from the beach. The first piece was damp. The second was salt-soaked. The third produced a smell like someone had set a wet boot on fire.\n\n\"Why won't it catch?\" he demanded.\n\n\"Because you're trying to burn the sea,\" I said.\n\n\"Very poetic. Not useful.\"\n\nIroh watched from the path.\n\n\"Would you like some dry kindling?\" he asked.\n\nMaui straightened.\n\n\"I can make fire! I've made fire in rain, on rocks, in front of crowds who were extremely grateful. These sticks are simply refusing to appreciate my résumé.\"\n\n\"I have no doubt. The question is whether you wish to make smoke first.\"\n\nMaui regarded the dark plume rising from his little heap of wood.\n\n\"Perhaps I do not.\"\n\nIroh set down the cups. He had come to discuss planting herbs along the sheltered wall, but Fen had asked him to wait until the runoff from the new beds was tested. The salt level had dropped, though not enough for every cutting he wanted to grow.\n\nWe had an hour before the measurement.\n\nIroh opened a tin.\n\n\"Tea?\"\n\nMaui eyed it.\n\n\"Is it strong?\"\n\n\"Strong enough for conversation.\"\n\n\"Then we'll need a bigger pot.\"\n\nI sat beside the cold fire. Maui reluctantly followed. Iroh handed him the brass timer, a small device with a dial and a faint ticking sound.\n\n\"What is this?\"\n\n\"A reminder that leaves require time in water.\"\n\nMaui turned the timer over.\n\n\"I could make the water hotter.\"\n\n\"Then you might burn the leaves.\"\n\n\"I could make the leaves tougher.\"\n\n\"I recommend asking them first.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nMaui looked between us.\n\n\"Are you two doing this on purpose?\"\n\n\"Very little in life is entirely unplanned,\" Iroh said.\n\nHe poured the water and set the timer.\n\nWhile we waited, Fen came down the path with the garden's salinity report. The sheltered bed was suitable for rosemary and sea kale, but the lower edge remained too brackish for the mint Iroh had hoped to plant.\n\n\"We'll keep the mint at Jasmine Steps,\" he said easily.\n\nMaui looked impressed.\n\n\"You're not angry?\"\n\n\"Why should I be?\"\n\n\"You carried it all the way here.\"\n\n\"The plant will be happier elsewhere.\"\n\nI looked at Maui.\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"Don't say it,\" he warned.\n\n\"I wasn't going to.\"\n\n\"You were thinking something about the orchard.\"\n\n\"I contain many thoughts.\"\n\nIroh's eyes crinkled.\n\n\"Then you have plenty of material for an evening.\"\n\nThe timer rang.\n\nMaui nearly dropped it.\n\n\"What kind of villain builds a bell that small?\" Maui demanded, glaring at Iroh's timer.\n\n\"The kind who expects tea to steep for four minutes,\" Iroh answered.\n\n\"Four minutes? I've held the sun still longer than that.\"\n\n\"You must have been terribly bored.\"\n\nMini Maui lay flat on his stomach across the tattoos, pantomiming sleep. Maui gave his own chest an offended look. Iroh was laughing into his cup when the distant memory of the lower coastal bell made us all fall quiet.\n\nMaui rolled the timer between his palms, suddenly gentle with it. His instinct was to run toward any sound that might mean danger. The idea of a locked passage waiting beneath the water plainly irritated him.\n\n\"All right,\" he said, working his grin back into place. \"No heroic demolition today. But if that old bell decides to challenge me personally, I'm accepting.\"\n\nIroh lifted his cup. \"Perhaps first we should discover whether it is a bell.\"\n\n\"You're both terrible for my reputation.\"\n\nHe drank the tea anyway. I could see that listening without doing anything spectacular was costing him more than he wanted us to know.\n\n\"That's a very small bell.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" Iroh said.\n\nThe word changed the air.\n\nWe had all heard the second bell beneath the cove several days earlier. It was still being measured. Fen had closed the lowest route while Danse and Tamsin examined the old structure. Neri had admitted the passage was sealed during the Closed Bells, though they still resisted naming everyone responsible.\n\nMaui looked toward the water.\n\n\"Could the old bell be like this?\"\n\n\"Perhaps,\" Iroh said. \"But a bell is only a bell until we know who placed it and why.\"\n\n\"That sounds less exciting than a sea spirit.\"\n\n\"It is also easier to inspect.\"\n\nI picked up my cup.\n\nThe tea tasted of toasted grain and citrus, the affordable house blend Iroh had developed after the poor mountain harvest. It wasn't the rare mint, and it didn't need to pretend it was.\n\n\"Good,\" I said.\n\nIroh smiled.\n\nMaui sipped, considered, and said, \"I could drink six cups.\"\n\n\"You may have one more.\"\n\n\"Why only one?\"\n\n\"Because you are already remarkably energetic.\"\n\nMaui laughed.\n\nFen came back from the drainage channel with mud on their sleeve and a request for two more buckets of clean water. The rosemary cuttings had been placed in the right bed, but the path to it was still slick.\n\nMaui started to leap toward the shed. Iroh lifted one hand.\n\n\"Perhaps we should ask where the buckets are wanted before we carry them.\"\n\nFen looked from one to the other. \"By the north path, not beside the stove. And don't pour them on the seedlings until I check the soil.\"\n\nMaui stopped. \"A man could lose his whole heroic reputation carrying water incorrectly.\"\n\n\"An alarming risk,\" Iroh said.\n\nI laughed and went to fetch the buckets with him. The two men watched one another with a sort of guarded curiosity. Maui clearly wanted to impress Iroh, who had survived battles and chosen to run a tea shop. Iroh seemed amused by Maui's inability to let a quiet moment remain quiet.\n\nLater, while the fire pit cooled, Iroh asked if I intended to stay at Tideglass that evening.\n\n\"I do,\" I said. \"I have time for tea with you tomorrow if you'd like, but I don't want either of you assuming I can be in two places at once.\"\n\nMaui picked up his cup. \"That seems a shame. I'd be magnificent at sharing a schedule with two Jaspers.\"\n\n\"One of me is more than enough.\"\n\nIroh chuckled. \"You have been very clear about preferring polyamory and ethical non-monogamy. An invitation is not diminished because it has to be accepted.\"\n\nMaui looked down at his own hands. \"I know that. Sometimes I'm still figuring out how to say I wish I had more time.\"\n\n\"Then say exactly that,\" I told him.\n\nHe met my eyes. \"I wish I had more time.\"\n\nThe simple answer made my throat tighten. I squeezed his hand beneath the table, and Iroh politely became interested in rescuing a cup from the breeze.\n\nWhen we rose to help clean the cooking area, neither man needed to prove himself better at warmth, wisdom or carrying stones. The tea had gone cold. The company hadn't.\n\nThe afternoon stretched out with ordinary work. We moved the dry rosemary cutting to the proper bed. I helped Fen check the drainage channel. Maui carried a crate of stones and, to his credit, did not turn the job into a challenge.\n\nBy evening the wind had shifted toward the shore. Iroh stayed to help us light a small cooking fire using properly dried wood and an ash pit set away from the growing beds. He explained the difference between a fire that warms people and one built to impress them.\n\n\"Do you ever want to impress someone?\" Maui asked.\n\nIroh looked at me.\n\n\"Frequently. Usually I make a terrible fool of myself.\"\n\nI felt my cheeks warm.\n\nMaui grinned.\n\n\"So do I.\"\n\nI stared at both of them.\n\n\"I have no idea why I spend time with either of you.\"\n\n\"Excellent judgment,\" Iroh said.\n\n\"Terrible taste,\" Maui added.\n\nThey laughed like men who had agreed not to compete for the evening. That mattered more to me than a perfect speech about how modern our relationships were.\n\nAfter supper, Iroh returned to Jasmine Steps. He had work in the morning and wanted time to prepare it. We walked him to the upper path.\n\n\"Thank you for the tea,\" I said.\n\n\"Thank you for the company.\"\n\nHe touched my hand, warm and gentle, and waited until I returned the gesture. Maui stood a respectful distance away, checking the lanterns along the path.\n\n\"I'll see you at the pantry tomorrow,\" I said.\n\n\"I look forward to it.\"\n\nIroh bowed his head to Maui.\n\n\"Be gentle with your fire.\"\n\n\"Only if it behaves.\"\n\nThey smiled at each other.\n\nWhen Iroh had gone, Maui fell into step beside me.\n\n\"He's good at waiting,\" he said.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"I'm trying.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nHe was quiet for a while. The smoke from the cooking fire lingered faintly in our clothes. We passed the garden, where the sea kale stood in neat rows and the dry-slope herbs tilted toward the last light.\n\n\"Can I say something foolish?\" he asked.\n\n\"Always.\"\n\n\"Sometimes I wonder how you can love more than one person and still know which way to walk home.\"\n\nI stopped.\n\n\"Do you mean tonight?\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\nI took his hand.\n\n\"I don't love Iroh because you're missing something. I don't love you because anyone else is missing something. I care about different people for different reasons, and I've chosen to be honest about that. Tonight I want to come home with you.\"\n\nHe studied my face.\n\n\"And tomorrow?\"\n\n\"Tomorrow I have tea with Iroh. And then I'll decide how to spend the rest of the day. The agreement isn't that everybody gets the same hours. It's that we tell each other what we can actually offer.\"\n\nMaui breathed out.\n\n\"That makes sense.\"\n\n\"Does it feel okay?\"\n\n\"Mostly.\"\n\n\"Mostly is a good beginning.\"\n\nHe smiled, a little shy.\n\n\"I want to be the person you can tell when mostly isn't enough.\"\n\n\"You already are.\"\n\nI stepped closer. He leaned down to meet me, and the kiss was warm and slow, tasting faintly of citrus tea. For once neither of us had a clever remark ready.\n\nWe walked home after the lanterns were lit.\n\nThe little brass timer stayed with Iroh. The second bell remained a mystery beneath the tide.\n\nI was learning that patience could be an act, not just the absence of movement.","word_count":1729},"maui-23":{"number":23,"title":"The Wrong Kind of Hero","content":"The trouble began when Aang said the festival arch looked a little plain.\n\nIt was a freestanding wooden frame at the edge of Lanternwell Square, built to hold winter lanterns without placing more weight on the ancient stone arches. Tamsin had measured it carefully. Danse had checked the joints. Pip had painted small signs explaining which path remained open during the preparations.\n\n\"Plain?\" Maui repeated.\n\nAang looked at the frame.\n\n\"Only a little. Perhaps we could add something that moves with the wind.\"\n\nMaui smiled.\n\nThat should have warned us.\n\nWithin an hour they had invented a plan involving twelve ribbons, six carved birds, a hanging model of a canoe, and a spinning wheel meant to symbolize the Threadways. Aang could keep the wheel turning with a gentle current of air. Maui could lift the heavy decorations into position without a ladder.\n\nI looked at the drawing.\n\n\"How much does all that weigh?\"\n\nMaui waved a hand.\n\n\"A heroic amount of not much.\"\n\n\"How much?\"\n\n\"Not enough to trouble me.\"\n\n\"That's not the measurement I asked for.\"\n\nAang laughed nervously.\n\n\"We'll check it.\"\n\nThey did not check it before beginning.\n\nI was helping Mara set up the baking stall when I heard the crack. Not a dramatic thunderclap. A short, brittle sound that came from wood under the wrong kind of strain.\n\nTamsin turned before I did.\n\n\"Stop!\"\n\nAang dropped the breeze. Maui caught the spinning wheel before it could fall. The top beam sagged, and one joint opened far enough to reveal the pale wood inside.\n\nNobody was underneath.\n\nNobody was hurt.\n\nWe were lucky.\n\nTamsin did not look grateful.\n\n\"Who approved the additional load?\" she asked.\n\nMaui lowered the wheel.\n\n\"I thought—\"\n\n\"That's not the answer.\"\n\nAang landed beside him.\n\n\"We wanted to make it beautiful.\"\n\n\"Beauty doesn't hold a beam together.\"\n\nI watched both of them shrink a little under the force of that sentence.\n\nTamsin ordered the area cleared. Danse arrived and measured the deflection. The hanging decorations came down. A sign went up across the path until the frame could be inspected.\n\nThe festival was scheduled for the following evening.\n\nSomebody would have to tell the volunteers the arch wasn't safe.\n\nAang looked miserable.\n\nMaui looked as though he wanted to pull the whole structure apart and rebuild it with his bare hands.\n\nI wanted to comfort them. I wanted to tell them the mistake had been small, that everyone knew they meant well.\n\nThen I looked at Pip, who had spent the morning checking clearances for people who needed the wider route.\n\nTheir work was now blocked.\n\n\"Who is going to explain the closure?\" I asked.\n\nAang looked at Maui.\n\nMaui looked at me.\n\n\"Both of you,\" I said.\n\nThey went to the notice board.\n\nIt wasn't a heroic speech. Aang explained that they had increased the load without permission and damaged the festival frame. Maui acknowledged that he'd ignored the structural limits because he could lift the decorations easily. They apologized to the volunteers whose work had been interrupted.\n\nPip raised a hand.\n\n\"Will the repairs be complete by tomorrow?\"\n\nTamsin answered.\n\n\"No.\"\n\nA few people groaned.\n\nMaui opened his mouth.\n\nI caught his eye.\n\nHe closed it.\n\nThe festival committee chose an alternative: lanterns on independent stands along the safe route, with the main frame closed until inspected and repaired. It would be smaller. It would also be something people could actually use.\n\nThat evening I found Maui and Aang beside the damaged beam.\n\nMaui was holding the damaged ribbon wheel at arm's length like evidence in a trial.\n\n\"A brilliant idea,\" he said, \"defeated by the smallest possible piece of wood.\"\n\nAang looked miserable. \"It was supposed to look like the wind carrying everyone home.\"\n\n\"It would have, if it hadn't tried to carry the arch with it.\"\n\nMini Maui swung from his upper arm and pointed dramatically at the sagging beam. Aang's staff leaned against the wall, and neither man could pretend the damage was imaginary.\n\n\"Can you fix it?\" Aang asked.\n\n\"Of course I can fix it. I can lift the whole thing.\"\n\n\"That's not a repair,\" I said.\n\n\"Well, it's a very *impressive* relocation.\"\n\nAang laughed despite himself. Then he asked whether Pip could decide what decoration still worked within the approved load. Maui groaned as if the plan were a personal attack, but he went to find Pip with Aang instead of hanging the wheel by force. He was still enjoying the idea of a magnificent festival. He was simply learning that the crowd's enjoyment didn't give him permission to break the stage.\n\nNeither looked pleased with himself.\n\n\"We were trying to make people happy,\" Aang said.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"It was a good idea,\" Maui added.\n\n\"Parts of it were.\"\n\nHe frowned.\n\n\"That isn't very encouraging.\"\n\n\"It isn't meant to be. You both promised something before checking whether the structure could take it.\"\n\nAang sat on a bench.\n\n\"I hate disappointing people.\"\n\nMaui nodded.\n\n\"I hate looking small.\"\n\nThe honesty surprised me.\n\nI sat between them.\n\n\"Then maybe the problem wasn't the decorations,\" I said. \"Maybe it was wanting the crowd to tell you you were good.\"\n\nMaui looked offended for a second.\n\nThen his expression changed.\n\nAang stared at his hands.\n\n\"I used to be the youngest person in every serious room,\" he said. \"People looked at me and wanted the Avatar to have an answer. Sometimes I learned to act certain before I was.\"\n\nMaui glanced at him.\n\n\"I've been doing that for centuries.\"\n\nI wanted to laugh. I didn't.\n\n\"You can both be useful without being impressive,\" I said.\n\nMaui folded his arms.\n\n\"That sounds dreadful.\"\n\n\"Try it.\"\n\nThe next morning they showed up at the square early, without costumes or fanfare.\n\nAang helped Pip carry the smaller lantern stands to their marked positions. Maui sorted hooks by size and replaced frayed cords. Tamsin supervised the repairs. Danse checked the temporary wiring and made sure the extension cables didn't cross the accessible path.\n\nI passed out tea from Iroh's new house blend.\n\nMara brought bread.\n\nToph walked over, tapped the damaged frame with one heel, and made a noise of disapproval.\n\n\"That,\" she said, \"is what happens when two show-offs think physics is optional.\"\n\nAang groaned.\n\nMaui looked as though he might challenge her to a competition.\n\nToph smiled.\n\n\"Don't.\"\n\nHe didn't.\n\nThe festival began at dusk.\n\nThe square looked different from the original plan. There was no giant spinning wheel. No hanging canoe. The decorations were modest enough to let the old stone arches remain the center of the space without bearing any extra load.\n\nChildren carried lanterns along the safe route. Edda sat near the fountain where the path was wide enough for people to pass without squeezing around her. Neri opened the celebration with a short reminder of the Closed Bells: the lights were meant to guide, never to trap.\n\nMaui stood beside Pip holding a spare bundle of hooks.\n\nAang helped a child whose lantern had gone out.\n\nNobody applauded them.\n\nI watched Maui glance toward the crowd, then back at the work in his hands.\n\nHe smiled anyway.\n\nAfter the festival, he met me beside the fountain.\n\n\"Well?\" he asked.\n\n\"Well what?\"\n\n\"Was I heroic?\"\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"You noticed the cord that was about to come loose, and you fixed it before anybody tripped. You helped Pip without taking over. You accepted the smaller celebration.\"\n\nHe waited.\n\n\"That was good work,\" I said.\n\nHe considered it.\n\n\"Not very heroic.\"\n\n\"Maybe you have the wrong definition.\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\nI took his hand and pulled him close enough to kiss.\n\n\"Can I have a reward?\" he murmured.\n\nI raised an eyebrow.\n\n\"Careful.\"\n\nHe winced.\n\n\"Right. Terrible word.\"\n\n\"You can ask for a kiss.\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\n\"May I kiss you?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nThe kiss was gentle at first, then a little warmer when I curled my fingers into the edge of his cloak. The night smelled of burnt wick and fresh bread. I could hear Aang laughing with Toph near the stalls, and the quiet music of Greg's guitar carried over the square.\n\nWhen we parted, Maui rested his forehead against mine.\n\n\"I think I like the smaller version,\" he said.\n\n\"Of the festival?\"\n\n\"Of me.\"\n\nI held his hand.\n\nWe walked home through the lanterns, leaving the damaged frame roped off for the repair team.\n\nIt was still broken.\n\nNobody needed us to pretend otherwise.","word_count":1405},"maui-24":{"number":24,"title":"A Place to Put Jealousy","content":"The letter was folded into the shape of a bird.\n\nIt had been left on the Lanternwell notice board with my name written in careful blue ink. Inside was an invitation to spend an afternoon at Moonroot with Iroh, reviewing tea-stained maps of the old terraces. He had drawn a tiny teacup at the bottom and, beneath it, written that the invitation was entirely optional.\n\nMaui read over my shoulder.\n\n\"A map date,\" he said.\n\n\"An archive visit.\"\n\n\"With tea.\"\n\n\"Those things can happen together.\"\n\nHe smiled. I waited for the joke. None came.\n\nWe were in Tideglass Cove a little later when he dropped the same length of rope twice. He wasn't usually clumsy with rope. That was why I noticed.\n\n\"Is your hand hurting again?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Then what's wrong?\"\n\nHe pulled the line through the ring and tightened it much harder than he needed to.\n\n\"Nothing,\" he announced with the unconvincing grandeur of a man preparing to fight a rope. \"Absolutely nothing. Except that your little folded-bird invitation keeps attacking my brain, which is an impressive feat for paper.\"\n\nI put down the tide chart.\n\n\"Maui.\"\n\nHis shoulders lowered.\n\n\"Can we talk somewhere people aren't measuring knots?\"\n\nWe walked to the little shelter above the sea-glass orchard. One raised bed was sprouting; the second was still bare because Fen had refused to transplant anything until the salt reading changed. The bench Greg and Maui had built sat beside the path, facing the water. It had no plaque, though Maui had carved a tiny fish beneath one armrest.\n\nBefore we reached the bench, Maui shifted into a hawk, circled once over the orchard, and landed on the roof with a flourish of feathers. The transformation wasn't necessary. I suspected it was the nearest available substitute for pacing.\n\nWhen he became himself again, his grin was enormous and a little desperate.\n\n\"That was a spectacular entrance to a conversation I am going to handle very maturely.\"\n\n\"Is this about Iroh?\"\n\n\"Wow. You ruin all my suspense.\"\n\nMini Maui jabbed a tiny finger at his chest. He brushed the tattoo aside.\n\n\"I know what we agreed,\" Maui said. \"I know you're ethically non-monogamous, and I know you prefer polyamory. I even know I agreed to try, which was extremely noble of me.\"\n\n\"Generous of you.\"\n\n\"Legendary.\" The joke fell short. He looked at the garden instead of at me. \"I still hate imagining someone else making you laugh in a way I don't know how to.\"\n\nHe didn't turn the admission into a demand. I wanted to comfort him, but I also wanted him to understand that my other relationships weren't something we were negotiating away. We sat with the discomfort between us, and he eventually nudged my shoe with his much larger one.\n\n\"So,\" he said, \"can I be jealous and still make you dinner?\"\n\n\"Absolutely.\"\n\n\"Great. My jealous cooking is famous.\"\n\nHe stood instead of sitting.\n\n\"I know about Iroh,\" he said.\n\n\"I know you do.\"\n\n\"I know about the others. We talked about being ethically non-monogamous. You told me that you prefer polyamory, and I said I wanted to try being part of your life without treating it as a race.\"\n\n\"You did.\"\n\n\"I meant it.\"\n\nI waited.\n\n\"But sometimes,\" he admitted, \"I hear that you've been invited somewhere, and part of me starts inventing a story where everybody else gets the best parts of you.\"\n\nThe wind lifted a strand of hair across my face.\n\n\"Which parts?\"\n\n\"The ones that aren't tired from fixing the village. The ones that laugh because someone said something clever and not because you're trying to make me feel better.\"\n\nHis voice was almost embarrassed.\n\nI wanted to tell him he was wrong. I wanted to assure him that I laughed more with him, or loved him more, or somehow held a private scale on which I could prove he wasn't losing.\n\nAny of those answers would have made the feeling smaller at the cost of making somebody else's love a competition.\n\n\"I laugh with you because you're funny,\" I said. \"Sometimes because you're ridiculous. Those are not consolation laughs.\"\n\nHis mouth twitched.\n\n\"Mostly ridiculous.\"\n\n\"Frequently.\"\n\nHe sat down at last.\n\n\"What do I do with this feeling? I don't want to tell you who you may see.\"\n\n\"You can tell me when you're afraid. You can ask for time together. You can ask whether a plan has changed. But I can't promise never to love anyone else, and I won't ask them to disappear to make us feel safe.\"\n\nMaui stared at the tide.\n\n\"I don't want that either.\"\n\n\"I believe you.\"\n\nHe looked up.\n\n\"You do?\"\n\n\"Yes. Wanting reassurance isn't the same thing as wanting control. It's what you do with it that matters.\"\n\nHe leaned back.\n\n\"That's inconvenient. I was hoping there'd be a monster to fight.\"\n\n\"Jealousy doesn't have claws, Maui.\"\n\n\"Mine might.\"\n\nI laughed, and this time he laughed with me.\n\nWe sat until the breeze grew cold. I told him I could make time for a meal after my archive visit, but only if I finished when I'd said I would and didn't overpromise to Iroh. He told me he wanted us to have a day with no errands, no celebrations, and no people asking him to carry things.\n\n\"A day of doing nothing?\" I asked.\n\n\"Not nothing.\"\n\n\"What, then?\"\n\n\"Whatever we choose after breakfast.\"\n\nThat sounded wonderful.\n\nWe put it on the calendar.\n\nAt Moonroot, the map Iroh had invited me to see was spread beneath a weighted sheet of glass. A corner had been damaged by an old leak, leaving the ink blurred where the terrace boundaries met.\n\n\"This is what I wanted your opinion on,\" he said. \"It appears someone redrew a garden path after the flood. I don't know whether the new route was ever used.\"\n\nI studied the line. The nearest record showed an invoice for gravel, but no confirmation that the work had been done. Iroh didn't ask me to solve it with a flash of remembered knowledge. He asked which evidence we should look for next.\n\nI thought of Maui's rope, dropped twice, and of how jealous he'd sounded without wanting to make me responsible for fixing the feeling.\n\n\"Can I tell you something without making it your problem?\" I asked.\n\n\"Certainly.\"\n\n\"Maui was afraid I would enjoy this afternoon so much that I'd stop wanting days with him.\"\n\nIroh set his cup down. \"Do you want me to reassure him?\"\n\n\"No. That's between us. I wanted you to know why I might seem distracted.\"\n\nHe nodded. \"Then we can be distracted together, briefly, and return to the map.\"\n\nWe sat in silence until I remembered the gravel invoice. Iroh smiled at the practical suggestion. It was one of the ways he differed from Maui: where Maui wanted to move the mountain, Iroh sometimes wanted to examine the date written at its foot.\n\n\"I like both of you,\" I said. \"In different ways. I've been explicit that I'm ethically non-monogamous and prefer polyamory. I don't want either affection turned into a test the other has to pass.\"\n\n\"Then perhaps we should both resist the temptation to be the most reassuring person in the room.\"\n\nI laughed. \"You make that sound easy.\"\n\n\"Oh, it isn't. I am extraordinarily good at giving advice nobody requested.\"\n\nHe touched my hand, then waited. I leaned closer, enough to tell him the invitation was welcome. We kissed briefly beside the map table before returning to the damaged record. Neither of us claimed to have fixed jealousy or the old terrace in one afternoon.\n\nOn my way to Moonroot, I stopped at the notice board. The old departure ledger had been updated with a sign explaining how travelers could request private help leaving without surrendering their destination. Somebody had placed a bowl of chalk beside it so visitors could mark whether they'd already received assistance, then erase the mark themselves.\n\nI thought about how much had changed since everyone treated the arrival book like a list of people the vale owned.\n\nAt Moonroot, Iroh greeted me with tea and a stack of maps.\n\n\"You look thoughtful,\" he said.\n\n\"Maui and I had a difficult conversation.\"\n\n\"Would you like to tell me?\"\n\n\"Some of it.\"\n\nHe listened without asking me to make a comparison between them. We examined the old terrace drains, discovered a marking that might indicate a seasonal overflow, and argued gently over whether a particular line was a road or a watercourse.\n\nWhen I returned to Tideglass, Maui was cooking. Properly dried kindling burned beneath a pot of vegetables, and he'd set two places at the table.\n\n\"How was your date?\" he asked.\n\n\"Good. We found a possible drainage mark.\"\n\n\"Romantic.\"\n\n\"In its way.\"\n\nHe studied me.\n\n\"Did you have a good time?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHis expression softened.\n\n\"Good.\"\n\nI went around the table and kissed his cheek.\n\nHe turned toward me, surprised.\n\n\"Was that a reassurance kiss?\"\n\n\"No,\" I said. \"That was a kiss because I wanted to kiss you.\"\n\n\"Important distinction.\"\n\n\"Extremely.\"\n\nHe asked if I wanted another.\n\nI did.\n\nWe burned the first piece of bread because neither of us remembered the stove. Later, with dinner rescued and the windows open to the sea, he told me he'd still felt jealous for a few minutes after I left.\n\n\"And?\"\n\n\"And then I went to see Fen about the ropes. I didn't make you come home early. I didn't ask Iroh to change his plans.\"\n\nI took his hand.\n\n\"That sounds like a good place to put the feeling.\"\n\n\"Where?\"\n\n\"Somewhere it can be heard without becoming the person in charge.\"\n\nHe considered that, then pulled me close enough that I could rest my forehead against his.\n\n\"Can I ask for something else?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"That day without errands.\"\n\n\"It's on the calendar.\"\n\n\"Can it start with sleeping late?\"\n\n\"Absolutely.\"\n\nHe grinned.\n\nThat night we closed the kitchen door, left the dishes soaking, and kept the remainder of our time private. The next morning the dishes were still there. So was the calendar.\n\nWe washed them together, and neither of us pretended we'd cured jealousy forever.","word_count":1693},"maui-25":{"number":25,"title":"Mereana Returns","content":"Mereana's boat arrived without a banner.\n\nThat was the first thing I noticed. The second was that she stepped onto the dock carrying a toolbox, not an instrument.\n\nMaui had been expecting a performance. He'd told three different people that the visiting artists from Aotearoa might be bringing a celebration to the cove, and by noon half the village was hovering near the landing.\n\nMereana looked at the crowd and raised one eyebrow.\n\n\"Did someone announce a festival?\"\n\nMaui looked at me. The grin he normally wore when a crowd gathered had frozen halfway across his face. \"Technically,\" he muttered, \"I may have advertised a magnificent artistic occasion before asking the artists whether they wanted one. A minor oversight. Possibly a large oversight.\"\n\nI looked at Maui.\n\n\"Apparently,\" I said, \"someone did.\"\n\nHer group had come to inspect a set of carved panels they had lent to the local heritage workshop. They were returning after an exhibition elsewhere, and Mereana had scheduled a short visit because she wanted to see how the panels were being stored and whether the agreement still suited her group.\n\nIt was their work. They could choose when to display it, discuss it, or take it home.\n\nPip hurried down the steps carrying a clipboard.\n\n\"We prepared the hall,\" they said. \"We can set up chairs if you'd like to perform.\"\n\n\"That's kind,\" Mereana answered. \"But we didn't come to perform.\"\n\nThe disappointment moved through the crowd like a breeze.\n\nA visitor standing near the rope store spoke before I could.\n\n\"But aren't you here to do the traditional courtship dance? Someone told me your group teaches the old way people choose partners.\"\n\nMereana went still.\n\n\"No,\" she said.\n\nThe visitor blinked.\n\n\"I read somewhere—\"\n\n\"Where?\"\n\n\"I don't remember.\"\n\n\"Then perhaps don't explain my people to me from a thing you can't remember.\"\n\nHer voice wasn't raised. She didn't need it to be.\n\nI felt my face grow hot on her behalf, which was a familiar and unhelpful sensation. I wanted to step in, make a speech about respect, and rescue everyone from the silence.\n\nMereana was perfectly capable of responding herself.\n\nI stayed beside the supply cart.\n\nThe visitor looked uncomfortable.\n\n\"I didn't mean offense.\"\n\n\"Then you can ask about what we're actually doing,\" Mereana said. \"We are artists. Some of our work draws on specific traditions with specific histories. Those aren't interchangeable with a universal romance ritual, and they aren't yours to assign us because you've come expecting entertainment.\"\n\nPip lowered the clipboard.\n\n\"Would you prefer we close the hall to the public?\"\n\n\"Not necessarily. I'd like to inspect the panels. Then we'll decide whether there's anything we want to share.\"\n\nMaui stepped forward.\n\nMini Maui popped up on his shoulder and raised both arms for the applause that wasn't coming. Maui gave him a long, injured stare.\n\n\"I may have been a tiny bit enthusiastic,\" he admitted.\n\nMereana folded her arms. \"You told half the cove we were doing a performance.\"\n\n\"That sounds worse when you say it.\"\n\n\"It should.\"\n\nHe glanced at me as if I might help him charm his way free. I raised my eyebrows. He sighed with the sort of theatrical suffering normally reserved for a cursed demigod.\n\n\"Okay. Here's what happened. I got excited, pictured a brilliant show, and forgot to ask the people who'd actually have to do it. Big mistake. Very on-brand mistake. Still mine.\"\n\nMereana studied him for a moment. \"We came to inspect the lending rack, Maui. The work is ours to plan.\"\n\n\"Then the famous Maui will provide only the services requested.\" He drew himself upright. \"Heavy objects moved. Doors held. Extraordinary smiling.\"\n\n\"Start with the doors,\" she said.\n\nHe bowed extravagantly. The apology was real, but so was his need to make somebody smile. Mereana didn't owe him a performance for trying.\n\n\"I told people you might perform. I shouldn't have.\"\n\nMereana looked at him.\n\n\"No, you shouldn't.\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\n\"I'll correct it.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\nThat could have been the end of the matter, except the panels had been moved.\n\nNot stolen. Not damaged deliberately. Someone had relocated them to a storage room where the air was drier, without telling Mereana's group. The new room was locked, and the person with the key was away at the market.\n\nMereana looked at the empty display rack.\n\n\"That wasn't our agreement.\"\n\nNeri arrived with the lending record and apologized. There was no excuse to invent. The workshop had made a decision about someone else's work without consulting its owners.\n\nWe found the keyholder. We inspected the storage room. Two panels were fine, one had warped slightly against a support that put pressure on its edge, and the fourth had been wrapped in fabric that trapped moisture.\n\nMereana took notes.\n\n\"Will it recover?\" I asked.\n\n\"Possibly. We need to examine it properly.\"\n\nThe workshop steward offered to pay for a repair.\n\nMereana nodded.\n\n\"That's part of it. The other part is changing the handling process.\"\n\nShe asked for a revised lending form that named who could move the work, under what conditions, and how the owners would be contacted. Neri agreed to help draft it. Tamsin volunteered to make a better storage rack, but only after Mereana approved the design.\n\nMaui carried the old rack outside.\n\nHe didn't make a speech.\n\nI found him by the door, watching the group examine the panels.\n\n\"Still wish it were a festival?\" I asked.\n\n\"A little.\"\n\n\"Me too.\"\n\nHe looked surprised.\n\n\"I like hearing people share beautiful things. I just keep forgetting that choosing not to share is part of the beauty too.\"\n\nI nodded.\n\nMereana accepted the tea, then asked whether the chairs could be moved to somewhere the wind wouldn't blow dust into the cups. We carried them into the workshop's small courtyard, where a canvas awning had been pulled taut between two posts.\n\n\"I was worried you'd leave angry,\" Maui admitted.\n\n\"I was angry,\" she answered. \"I also wanted the panels checked. Both things can be true.\"\n\nHe looked at her over the rim of his cup.\n\n\"The work means something to the people who made it,\" she continued. \"Some of them are here, some couldn't travel. I was entrusted to speak about the loan. That doesn't make me the owner of everyone's story.\"\n\nNeri returned with a draft form. Mereana read the first page and pointed out that it included permission to photograph the panels for publicity. Her group had agreed to conservation photographs, not promotional images.\n\nNeri crossed out the sentence and wrote two separate options.\n\nMaui watched. \"I thought the village would be honored to display them.\"\n\n\"It was,\" Mereana said. \"But honor isn't the same as permission.\"\n\nWhen she excused herself to speak to the other artists, he slumped in his chair.\n\n\"I wanted them to feel celebrated.\"\n\n\"I know. Next time ask what celebration they want.\"\n\nHe laughed without much humor. \"Your next sentence is going to be that I don't get to choose the music.\"\n\n\"You can choose what music you play yourself.\"\n\nHe considered that, then drew a small circle on the table with his fingertip.\n\n\"Will you still dance with me after I embarrass myself?\"\n\n\"If you ask me and I want to.\"\n\nHe looked up, relief plain on his face. \"I will ask.\"\n\nWe helped carry the repaired rack back inside after Mereana approved its position. She checked the supports herself. The next morning's signed form would matter more than anything Maui could have announced from a stage, but the repaired storage space was a kind of welcome too.\n\nA little later Mereana joined us outside. She seemed tired, but not angry enough to leave immediately.\n\n\"Would you like tea?\" I asked.\n\n\"Yes. And a chair.\"\n\nI brought both.\n\nShe sat beneath the awning and watched the harbor.\n\n\"I don't mind people being curious,\" she said. \"I mind when curiosity arrives with a costume already picked out for me.\"\n\nMaui looked at his hands.\n\n\"I've done that to people.\"\n\n\"We all can,\" she said. \"The question is whether we notice, and what we do afterward.\"\n\nI thought of my own missing memories, the way strangers sometimes decided who I must have been from a few fragments. I understood the shape of her frustration without pretending the histories were the same.\n\nMereana turned toward me.\n\n\"How is the new garden?\"\n\n\"Small. Salty. Stubborn.\"\n\n\"Sounds like a place worth seeing.\"\n\nWe walked there together after the storage inspection. Fen explained the beds and how visitors could contribute without importing plants that damaged the dunes. Mereana was delighted by the little flowers growing untouched beside the signal post.\n\n\"Those were here before the beds?\" she asked.\n\n\"Yes,\" Fen said.\n\n\"Good. I like a place that leaves room for what was already living.\"\n\nBy sunset, the visiting group had decided to stay one more day to work on the storage agreement. They did not perform. Nobody pressured them.\n\nMaui cooked supper. Mereana told us about a boat repair she had supervised back home, mostly to complain about how much easier it was to give advice than to keep a project on budget. Maui confessed he was learning the same thing.\n\nShe laughed.\n\nWhen the meal ended, she stood and stretched.\n\n\"Thank you for asking what we wanted,\" she said.\n\nI looked at the untouched instruments beside her bag.\n\n\"Thank you for trusting us enough to tell us no.\"\n\nAfter she left, Maui and I washed the cups.\n\n\"Was that a successful visit?\" he asked.\n\n\"She got her work inspected, the process changed, and she chose to stay. I'd say yes.\"\n\n\"No performance.\"\n\n\"No performance.\"\n\nHe turned toward me.\n\n\"Can I sing?\"\n\nI stared at him.\n\n\"No.\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\nI kissed him before he could begin anyway.\n\nThe following morning, the lending form had new boxes for consent to move, display, repair, and return the panels. None were checked by default.\n\nMereana read it, made three corrections, and signed only the parts she'd agreed to.\n\nThat was the kind of welcome worth keeping.","word_count":1671},"maui-26":{"number":26,"title":"The Price of Welcome","content":"The pantry shelves looked fuller from the doorway.\n\nIt was an optical illusion caused by the jars in the front row. Behind them, the flour sack was nearly empty, the last dried beans had been marked for the clinic, and the vegetable crates contained more leaves than vegetables.\n\nMara stood with the inventory sheet in her hand.\n\n\"Don't say it's fine,\" she warned.\n\n\"I wasn't going to.\"\n\n\"Everyone does.\"\n\nI stepped beside her and looked at the figures.\n\nA flood upstream had blocked the lower cart route for several days. Deliveries were using the longer ridge path. The pantry had fed the repair crews, provided emergency meals for two visiting families, and supplied the clinic volunteers without charging people who had arrived with nothing.\n\nNobody had done anything wrong by needing food.\n\nBut the jars didn't refill because we had been kind.\n\nMaui appeared carrying his hook.\n\n\"I'll fish. Give me an afternoon and a hook, and I will personally negotiate with every fish in this cove. Their negotiator will be extremely handsome.\"\n\nMara looked at him.\n\n\"How many?\"\n\n\"As many as we need.\"\n\n\"Who cleans them? Who salts them? Who stores them? Who makes sure the people who can't eat fish have something else?\"\n\nMaui blinked.\n\n\"Fish is food.\"\n\n\"Not the only food.\"\n\nI heard Katara's voice behind us.\n\n\"And a fresh catch won't survive a week in warm weather without proper handling.\"\n\nShe had come to check the clinic supplies and brought two volunteers with her. One of them had written down the meals people could actually eat, including allergies and religious or personal restrictions they had chosen to disclose.\n\nKatara put the sheet on the table.\n\n\"Let's start with what the families need. Then we can decide which supplies will help.\"\n\nMaui looked disappointed.\n\n\"Fishing still helps.\"\n\n\"Of course it does,\" Katara said. \"If the work after catching the fish is planned.\"\n\nThat was fair. It was also not a thrilling beginning to an emergency.\n\nWe called a pantry meeting.\n\nMara brought the ledger. Fen brought the tide chart and information about the safest fishing hours. Tamsin described which carts could use the ridge. Iroh offered an inexpensive soup recipe that used less fuel than several smaller meals. Greg volunteered to help distribute food but asked to be given a definite shift rather than a promise to appear whenever needed.\n\nEdda spoke about delivery access. Some families could reach the square, others couldn't. A meal on a high shelf wasn't useful to a person who couldn't lift it down.\n\nI made a list of offers.\n\nThen I caught myself assigning names to jobs.\n\n\"Wait,\" I said.\n\nEveryone looked up.\n\n\"I've written Greg on the evening delivery without asking whether he's free.\"\n\nGreg raised a hand.\n\n\"I'm not. I said tomorrow morning.\"\n\nI crossed out the shift.\n\n\"Sorry.\"\n\nMara grinned.\n\n\"Congratulations. You caught yourself before we had to.\"\n\nThe meeting took an hour. We planned three days of meals, set aside the clinic's medically necessary supplies, and wrote a public notice explaining which items were short without listing which families had requested help.\n\nMaui agreed to fish with Fen during the safe tide window. Two people who knew food handling volunteered to prepare the catch, and Mara allocated salt from the bakery stores with a clear repayment plan. The pantry would buy vegetables from the next available cart rather than assume someone would donate them.\n\nNone of the numbers were comfortable.\n\nThey were workable.\n\nThe next morning I went with Maui to the landing.\n\nHe was quiet while Fen checked the lines. The sea was calm, but the water carried branches and silt from the upstream flood. We used the upper route and kept the boat close to shore.\n\nMaui caught three fish.\n\n\"Three,\" he announced. \"A magnificent haul. Everyone will remember the day the great Maui conquered a terrifying school of—\"\n\nHe looked at the smallest fish and stopped.\n\n\"Well. Not everybody.\"\n\nMini Maui frowned at the little fish, then pantomimed sending it back. Maui sighed, loosened his grip, and lowered it into the water.\n\n\"You're ruining my legend,\" he told his tattoo.\n\nFen, at the bow, didn't look remotely impressed. \"You're keeping the nursery stocked. That's what we agreed.\"\n\n\"Terrible title for a song.\"\n\n\"Good title for another fishing season.\"\n\nMaui watched the little shape vanish beneath the surface. He wanted a story large enough to fit a hero. I could see him calculating the cost of obeying a limit no stranger would ever applaud.\n\nThen he flashed me a grin. \"All right. Two fish and one spectacular act of restraint. I expect full credit.\"\n\n\"You'll get an accurate count,\" I said.\n\n\"How romantic.\"\n\nHe helped Fen bring the boat in without sneaking another catch. The performance was ridiculous. The restraint wasn't.\n\nThen he stopped.\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"Only three?\"\n\n\"Fen says the small ones need to stay.\"\n\nFen nodded.\n\n\"Breeding season. Take the adults, leave the young, and we might still have fish next month.\"\n\nMaui looked at the water.\n\n\"I used to think feeding people meant bringing home the biggest possible catch.\"\n\n\"And now?\"\n\n\"Now it means making sure the sea can feed them again.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\nWe took the fish to the cleaning station, where Mara's cousin was waiting. Maui offered to help with the work. She handed him an apron.\n\nHe stared at it.\n\n\"You want me to wear that?\"\n\n\"Unless you enjoy smelling like fish for three days.\"\n\nHe put it on.\n\nBy afternoon the first meals were ready. The clinic received its share. The two visiting families chose from the available options, without having to explain publicly why they needed help. Edda checked that the delivery station was accessible from the level path.\n\nIroh asked Mara whether she wanted more soup tomorrow. She studied the pantry list before answering.\n\n\"Not unless you can bring clean jars back,\" she said. \"We don't have enough containers for another large delivery.\"\n\n\"Then I shall bring the soup in what I have and collect the empties after.\"\n\nMara nodded. I watched how the exchange left both people free to decide what they could actually provide. There was no miracle pot that filled itself, and Iroh didn't pretend his tea shop could feed a valley forever.\n\nA child arrived with a note from the clinic, asking whether the next delivery could include something easier to carry up the hill. Edda checked the route before we agreed to a new time. Maui looked at the note and then toward the sea.\n\n\"I could deliver it in one trip,\" he said.\n\n\"Probably,\" I answered. \"But can the next volunteer do the same?\"\n\nHe made a face. \"You're ruining all the fun.\"\n\n\"Just the kind that requires you to be here every time.\"\n\nWe divided the delivery into smaller loads that ordinary people could carry. Mara recorded the change; a new worker would be able to follow it without having to guess what we'd intended.\n\nWhen we finished, the apron around Maui's waist was damp and smelled terrible. I couldn't stop smiling at him.\n\n\"What?\" he demanded.\n\n\"Nothing. You're a fearsome fisher.\"\n\n\"I am a legend.\"\n\n\"With fish scales in your hair.\"\n\nHe tried to brush them away and made it worse. I laughed so hard I had to hold the edge of the table.\n\nHis eyes softened. \"I like making you laugh.\"\n\n\"I like laughing.\"\n\n\"Can I kiss you before the work starts again?\"\n\nI nodded, and he leaned close enough that I could smell salt and clean soap beneath the fish. The kiss was brief, gentle, and decidedly less heroic than anything he'd planned for the morning.\n\nThen Mara called us back to the loading list. The pantry remained short on flour, but its deliveries had a plan that didn't require our presence at every meal. It felt like a better kind of generosity.\n\nIroh brought a pot of soup.\n\nToph tasted it and declared it \"suspiciously sensible.\"\n\nKatara laughed.\n\nThe pantry wasn't saved forever. A storm could block the ridge again. A harvest could fail. Someone could need more help than we'd planned.\n\nThat was why we wrote down what worked and what didn't.\n\nAfter dinner, Mara counted the remaining stock.\n\n\"We have enough for the next three days,\" she said.\n\n\"Just three?\"\n\n\"Three is real. A week would be a promise we haven't earned.\"\n\nI looked at the figures.\n\nFor once, I didn't try to make them prettier.\n\nMaui found me at the table after the others left.\n\n\"You're worried.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"About the pantry?\"\n\n\"About how often welcome means somebody quietly doing more than they can afford.\"\n\nHe sat beside me.\n\n\"I thought I could solve it by fishing all night.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"I like doing something big.\"\n\n\"You also wore an apron today.\"\n\nHe groaned.\n\n\"Do not tell anyone.\"\n\n\"I have already told three people.\"\n\n\"Jasper.\"\n\nI laughed and leaned against him.\n\nHe turned his face toward mine.\n\n\"Would you like me to stay with you tonight?\" he asked.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Quietly?\"\n\n\"Mostly.\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\nHe kissed me softly, then kissed me again when I caught his sleeve. There was flour on my hands and a smear of salt on his wrist. I had never found a pantry inventory particularly romantic before.\n\nBut there was something tender about a person willing to do the ordinary part of caring.\n\nThe next morning we went back to the square to help unload the ridge cart. It was late, muddy, and carrying fewer vegetables than we'd hoped.\n\nMara looked at the crates and sighed.\n\nThen she opened the ledger.\n\nWe began again.","word_count":1596},"maui-27":{"number":27,"title":"The Visitor's Right to Leave","content":"The boy was sitting on his suitcase when I found him.\n\nHe couldn't have been more than nineteen, though his boots were worn in the way boots get when someone has been walking without much choice about where. His name was Tomas. He'd arrived through the northern Threadway three days ago and spent most of that time helping at the pantry. Now he was waiting on the steps beneath the arch.\n\nBeside him stood a woman named Vel, who had started calling Maui the Guardian of Tideglass after seeing him catch a boat during the mooring accident.\n\n\"You don't understand,\" Vel was telling him. \"People made room for you here. You can't just leave after they've helped.\"\n\nTomas stared at his shoes.\n\n\"I need to go home.\"\n\n\"Then why come at all?\"\n\nI stopped close enough to be heard without coming between them.\n\n\"Because people are allowed to visit,\" I said.\n\nVel turned toward me.\n\n\"The community fed him.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"And gave him a bed.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"And now he just walks out?\"\n\nI looked at Tomas.\n\n\"Do you want help reaching your door?\"\n\nHe nodded without looking up.\n\n\"All right. I'll check the safe route.\"\n\nVel put a hand on the suitcase handle.\n\n\"Someone should tell Maui.\"\n\nI felt my shoulders tighten.\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"He's the one who welcomed the newcomers. He'll understand.\"\n\nTomas pulled his suitcase back.\n\n\"Please don't make me explain to him.\"\n\nIt was barely louder than the sea.\n\nI took a breath. Then I asked Vel to release the handle. She did, reluctantly.\n\n\"Neri has a private departure service,\" I said. \"No one has to explain their reasons to a crowd.\"\n\nVel looked hurt.\n\n\"I thought this was a place where people cared.\"\n\n\"It is. Caring doesn't make the doors ours.\"\n\nI escorted Tomas to the notice board. The departure instructions were clear, but somebody had hung a festival banner across half the text. I moved it aside. Neri was on duty and listened as Tomas asked for a route without giving an address.\n\n\"Would you like a guide to the arch?\" they asked.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Would you like us to keep your destination private?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Then that's what we'll do.\"\n\nMaui arrived before we left, striding down the steps with his hook over one shoulder as though the ferry departure were an adventure he'd been promised. He saw Tomas's packed suitcase, and the grin he had brought for the crowd faltered. \"Okay,\" he said, turning the hook over in his hands. \"Tell me which part of this requires an enormous hero. I may need the answer slowly.\"\n\nVel straightened when she saw him. I saw the moment he recognized that look: pure, uncomplicated admiration, the kind he'd chased across oceans.\n\n\"Guardian of Tideglass,\" she said. \"Tell him he can't just take everything and go.\"\n\nMaui drew himself to full height. Mini Maui struck a triumphant pose over his heart. For half a second the temptation was visible. A grand speech, a grateful crowd, an audience that knew his name.\n\nThen he saw Tomas's hands clenched around the suitcase.\n\n\"Whoa,\" he said. \"Hold on. I rescue people. I don't collect them.\"\n\nVel frowned. \"But you made this place safe.\"\n\n\"With about a hundred other people, and several very angry clipboards.\" He grinned, then let the grin fade. \"That doesn't give me a vote on where Tomas goes next.\"\n\nMini Maui lowered his arms and looked mildly disappointed. Maui looked more disappointed still. He'd wanted to be somebody's guardian; being the person who opened a path without knowing whether he'd see them again was harder.\n\nWhen Tomas thanked him, Maui made a spectacular show of accepting the compliment. He didn't ask for a promise in return.\n\nNot because I'd summoned him. Vel had.\n\nHe came down the square with his hook over one shoulder and a confused expression.\n\n\"What's happening?\"\n\nVel stepped toward him.\n\n\"Tell him he should stay. We welcomed him.\"\n\nMaui looked at the suitcase, then at Tomas, then at me.\n\nI didn't rescue him from the choice.\n\n\"Tomas,\" he said, \"do you want to go home?\"\n\nTomas nodded.\n\n\"Then do you need anything for the journey?\"\n\nVel stared.\n\n\"But the pantry—\"\n\n\"The pantry helped because he needed help,\" Maui said. \"Not because we bought another member of the village.\"\n\nHe said it plainly. No thunder. No boast.\n\nVel looked at him like he'd broken something she loved.\n\n\"I thought you understood loyalty.\"\n\n\"I do.\"\n\n\"Then why aren't you asking him to stay?\"\n\n\"Because I wouldn't want someone to keep me somewhere by calling it gratitude.\"\n\nTomas looked up for the first time.\n\n\"Thank you,\" he whispered.\n\nWe walked him to the arch along the safe route. Neri came too, carrying the travel map. The Threadway glowed faintly in the stone, not bright enough for a spectacle. Tomas put his suitcase down, checked that he had his papers, and asked whether he could come back someday.\n\n\"If you want,\" Neri said.\n\nHe looked at Maui.\n\nMaui smiled.\n\n\"We'll still have terrible rope and decent bread.\"\n\nTomas laughed.\n\nThen he stepped through.\n\nThe light settled. The arch remained open for other travelers.\n\nVel had followed us at a distance. When Tomas disappeared, she turned away without speaking.\n\nAfter Tomas left, Neri took the public arrival register back to the office. They didn't cross out his name as though he'd never been there. They marked the departure as confirmed, with no destination printed for curious strangers.\n\nA small girl came up to the notice and asked whether Tomas had gone away because he didn't like the village.\n\n\"Maybe he missed home,\" I said. \"He was allowed to go even if he liked us very much.\"\n\nShe considered that. \"My grandmother goes home after tea.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Nobody gets mad at her.\"\n\n\"Exactly.\"\n\nThe girl ran off toward the bakery. I wished I could explain the matter as simply to the adults.\n\nMaui watched Neri lock the restricted ledger.\n\n\"I almost told Tomas he should stay until the next feast,\" he said.\n\n\"Would that have made him happier?\"\n\n\"I don't know. It would have made me feel better.\"\n\nThat was a difficult admission. I took his hand while we walked toward the river path.\n\n\"You're allowed to miss someone after they leave,\" I said. \"You don't get to make the missing into a duty they owe you.\"\n\nHe rubbed his thumb across my knuckles. \"Does that apply when you travel to see other people you love?\"\n\n\"Yes. I've told you I'm ethically non-monogamous and prefer polyamory. That doesn't mean I'll never be sad when we part. It means we can be honest without turning the sadness into a chain.\"\n\n\"I might need to be reminded.\"\n\n\"You can ask.\"\n\nWe passed Vel beside the market. She was still angry, and I was still angry about what she'd said to Tomas. I didn't approach her to demand an apology on his behalf. She had a right to understand her part in it at her own pace, even if I wished she'd start immediately.\n\nAt home, Maui put the returned carving on the shelf. I watched him choose not to throw it away.\n\n\"What if Tomas never comes back?\" he asked.\n\n\"Then the carving can still remind you that you cared.\"\n\nHe nodded. The little fish sat crookedly against the wall, plainly unfinished. Neither of us turned it around to make it look perfect.\n\nMaui watched her go.\n\n\"That hurt her,\" he said.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"I wanted her to understand.\"\n\n\"She might. Later. Or she might not.\"\n\nHe stared at the stones.\n\n\"I used to think having people admire me meant I had done something good. Now I know I can do something good and lose their admiration anyway.\"\n\nI touched his hand.\n\n\"That isn't a comfortable lesson.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nWe returned to Tideglass at dusk. Fen was setting up the tide chart for the next morning, and the first lanterns were flickering along the ridge. Maui offered to help carry the stand. Fen accepted without ceremony.\n\nFor a while that was enough.\n\nThe next day Vel arrived at our door with the little wooden fish Maui had once given her. She held it out.\n\n\"I don't want this anymore,\" she said.\n\nMaui took it.\n\n\"I'm sorry it upset you.\"\n\n\"It didn't upset me. It made me think you were someone who would always protect us.\"\n\n\"I will help when I can.\"\n\n\"That isn't the same.\"\n\n\"No,\" he said.\n\nShe left.\n\nHe turned the fish over in his hand. It was badly carved, with one eye much larger than the other. I remembered him making it on a rainy afternoon and declaring it a masterpiece.\n\n\"Want me to tell you she was wrong?\" I asked.\n\nHe shook his head.\n\n\"Not right now.\"\n\nWe sat on the steps.\n\n\"I liked being the person she thought I was,\" he admitted. \"Even though he didn't exist.\"\n\nI leaned against his shoulder.\n\n\"People can be disappointed when you don't belong to their story.\"\n\n\"Even you?\"\n\n\"Especially me. That's why I have to keep asking what you want instead of deciding what your legend means.\"\n\nHe put the fish beside the door.\n\n\"Do you still want me?\"\n\nI turned toward him.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Even without a crowd?\"\n\n\"Preferably without a crowd.\"\n\nHe laughed, the sound small and tired.\n\nI kissed him, gently, then pulled back when he seemed to need room to breathe.\n\nThat evening he put the returned fish on the shelf beside the green notebook from my old room. Two objects from people who had wanted different things. Neither one had to be thrown away to make the lesson disappear.\n\nIn the morning I checked the departure notice.\n\nThe festival banner had been moved to another wall.\n\nSomeone had written beneath the instructions, in ordinary dark ink:\n\nLeaving is not a debt.\n\nI didn't know who had written it.\n\nI was glad someone had.","word_count":1644},"maui-28":{"number":28,"title":"The Spare Key","content":"Neri offered me the key in a velvet pouch.\n\nThat was how I knew something was wrong.\n\nNeri was the kind of person who wrapped fragile archive seals in plain paper and put their own lunch in a tin that had lost its lid. Velvet meant ceremony, and ceremony usually meant somebody was trying to make a decision look less like a decision.\n\n\"What's that?\" I asked.\n\n\"A spare key to the lower records cabinet.\"\n\n\"Why do you want me to have it?\"\n\n\"Because you ask good questions.\"\n\nI waited.\n\n\"And because,\" Neri continued, \"the maintenance records may be important to understanding the second bell. If I am unavailable, someone should be able to open the cabinet.\"\n\nThe cabinet stood in Moonroot Archive, beneath a narrow window that let in just enough light to reveal the dust on its handles. Inside were copies of the old service-route plans, private correspondence whose release had not been authorized, and the burned ledger with the scratched-out signature.\n\nI looked at the pouch.\n\n\"Who else has a key?\"\n\nNeri blinked.\n\n\"Me.\"\n\n\"Who knows you're giving me one?\"\n\nThey hesitated.\n\nThat answered the question.\n\nI set the pouch down on the table.\n\n\"I can't take it like this.\"\n\nNeri looked hurt.\n\n\"I trust you.\"\n\n\"I know. I'm grateful. But trust isn't the same as authority.\"\n\nSeverus was cataloging a collection in the next room. He didn't turn around, though I could tell by the way his pen stopped that he'd heard every word.\n\nNeri folded their hands.\n\n\"Someone has to be able to open the cabinet in an emergency.\"\n\n\"Then we need an emergency procedure. Not a secret transfer of a key to somebody you like.\"\n\nThey looked toward the window.\n\n\"I thought it would be simpler.\"\n\n\"It would be simpler for us. Not for the people whose records are in there.\"\n\nI heard footsteps behind me.\n\nSeverus appeared in the doorway.\n\n\"An inconvenient distinction,\" he said.\n\nNeri sighed.\n\n\"You two are impossible.\"\n\n\"Correct,\" Severus replied.\n\nWe brought the question to the archive committee. The meeting took place in the same room where Severus's private letters had once been opened without permission. The memory made everyone careful, which was one good use of discomfort.\n\nThe committee chair, Vale, argued that the cabinet should have two keyholders.\n\n\"Two isn't an accountability system,\" I said. \"It's a number.\"\n\nTamsin, invited because the old service records contained structural plans, drew a diagram of the cabinet's contents and the reasons somebody might need access. Public maintenance documents could be copied and held openly. Private correspondence required separate handling. Emergency structural records needed a process that did not depend on Neri being awake.\n\nFen added a note about flood conditions.\n\n\"If water is rising,\" they said, \"we can't wait for an argument about who has the right to read a tide chart.\"\n\nWe separated the records into categories. The tide charts and structural inspection drawings would be accessible to the emergency team with a logged sign-out. Private letters would remain sealed unless their recipients authorized inspection or a narrowly documented immediate danger justified it.\n\nThe burned ledger would remain available to the evidence team under supervision. Nobody could scrape it further in pursuit of a satisfying name.\n\nThen we designed the key record.\n\nIt was a simple page: the date, the person opening the cabinet, the specific reason, which documents were accessed, whether copies were made, and when everything was returned.\n\nNo romantic histories. No traveler destinations. No list of who had looked worried when somebody arrived.\n\nMaui came to the meeting late, carrying a basket of bread from Mara.\n\nHe entered as if the archive meeting were the final scene of a great battle.\n\n\"Fear not! The mighty Maui has brought bread! No one is allowed to start a complicated meeting on an empty stomach. That's an ancient rule I just invented. Applause is optional, but encouraged.\"\n\nNeri took the basket from him. \"You're late.\"\n\n\"The bread was in danger of being eaten before I arrived. By me.\"\n\nMini Maui acted out the tragedy of a missing bun across his arm. Danse looked at the bread, then at the complicated cabinet sketch, clearly trying to decide which deserved more attention.\n\nMaui leaned over the diagram. \"So nobody has to guard it alone?\"\n\n\"Exactly,\" I said.\n\n\"Good. Being the only hero in the room is exhausting. Trust me, I'm exceptionally qualified.\"\n\nHe grinned as though he'd made a joke. I knew he'd meant the second half. The cabinet wouldn't make anybody indispensable; that was the point.\n\n\"Two keys, then?\" he asked. \"And a log?\"\n\n\"With the people who use them choosing the schedule,\" Neri answered.\n\nMaui shrugged as if the paperwork had personally insulted him. Then he carried the printer's next parcel to the shelf without touching the sealed papers inside. His instincts hadn't vanished. He was practicing what to do with them.\n\nHe looked at the diagram.\n\n\"Can we put two locks on it?\" he asked.\n\nDanse, who had come for the structural records, looked up.\n\n\"That might increase security.\"\n\nEdda cleared her throat.\n\n\"Or make it harder for someone who can't reach the top latch.\"\n\nDanse winced.\n\nMaui put the bread down.\n\n\"Right. Not that.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nAfter three hours, the committee approved a revised process. Neri would continue as archive steward. Severus would supervise evidence handling in his assigned hours. Tamsin and Fen could request emergency structural access using the public log. A second key would be kept in a sealed case in an accessible location, with a tamper-evident record.\n\nNeri folded the velvet pouch and tucked it beneath the minutes. I could see the disappointment in their face. They had wanted to trust me with something important, and my refusal had made them wonder whether I trusted them back.\n\n\"Could we go through what would happen if there were a flood tonight?\" I asked.\n\nThey looked up. \"Of course.\"\n\nWe traced the process on the updated chart. Fen would report the water level to the public emergency lead. Tamsin could request a structural inspection through the documented route. The archive steward would authorize access to the maintenance plans, and the request would be logged even if the decision had to be made urgently.\n\n\"What if you're asleep?\" I asked Neri.\n\n\"Then the named deputy answers. That's what we forgot to write down.\"\n\nOrin added the deputy contact to the restricted procedure. Edda checked that reaching the log didn't require using an inaccessible high latch. Severus corrected a phrase that would have let anyone claiming urgency see private letters unrelated to the storm.\n\nMaui leaned against the doorway. \"So no single person can be trapped being the hero of the cabinet.\"\n\n\"Precisely,\" Neri said.\n\nHe looked pleased. I knew him well enough to recognize the wish to give a grand speech about not holding keys anymore.\n\nInstead he said, \"Would anybody like bread?\"\n\nWe all did.\n\nLater Neri found me by the fountain and apologized for offering me a responsibility I'd never asked for.\n\n\"I know you thought it was an honor,\" I said. \"But honor can become a weight when it isn't chosen.\"\n\n\"Then I will ask next time.\"\n\n\"Thank you.\"\n\nI went home with Maui and told him that being trusted didn't have to mean being indispensable. He looked at the tide chart beside our door.\n\n\"I'm still learning that one too.\"\n\nHe took my hand and waited before leaning closer. I welcomed the kiss, glad it was something we could choose without a committee or a spare key.\n\nI wasn't made keeper of anything.\n\nI felt relieved.\n\nWhen the meeting ended, Neri waited by the door.\n\n\"I thought you were the right person,\" they said.\n\n\"To hold the key?\"\n\n\"To carry the responsibility.\"\n\nI looked at the pouch, still on the table.\n\n\"Maybe I can help without owning the decision.\"\n\nNeri smiled.\n\n\"You already did.\"\n\nOutside, the rain had started again. The old stone under Lanternwell Square smelled cool and mineral, and water ran down the channels toward Tideglass.\n\nMaui walked beside me.\n\n\"Do you ever wish somebody would just give you a mysterious key and tell you you're chosen?\"\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"Sometimes.\"\n\nHe grinned.\n\n\"I could make you one.\"\n\n\"Please don't.\"\n\n\"Golden? With a jewel?\"\n\n\"Absolutely not.\"\n\n\"Three jewels?\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nHe took my hand.\n\nWe passed the northern arch, where the departure notice had been updated. Then we stopped at the fountain to watch Neri's team install the new public sign-out board.\n\nThe process wasn't perfect. Somebody would forget a signature someday. An emergency could still force a difficult judgment. But at least everyone would know what the key opened and who was answerable for using it.\n\nMaui looked toward the water.\n\n\"There's still a key you wear,\" he said.\n\nI touched the brass shape at my neck, the one that fit the old maintenance hatches.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Do you think it means you should open them?\"\n\n\"No. I think it means I can help when the people affected have made a safe plan.\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\n\"That isn't much of a legend.\"\n\n\"It'll have to do.\"\n\nHe leaned close enough to kiss my forehead.\n\n\"Maybe the best legends are the ones where nobody gets trapped.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\n\"Write that down before you forget it.\"\n\nHe pulled a scrap of paper from his pocket and did, in enormous letters that took up half the page.\n\nLater, at home, we hung the scrap beside the tide chart.\n\nIt was a terrible piece of calligraphy.\n\nIt was a very good rule.","word_count":1587},"maui-29":{"number":29,"title":"A Festival Without a Hero","content":"The children wanted to build a stage.\n\nMaui wanted to build them a magnificent stage.\n\nTamsin wanted to know who would be responsible when the magnificent stage collapsed.\n\nWe were back where we'd started.\n\n\"The Tidewake festival needs something special,\" Maui insisted, spreading his arms toward the shoreline.\n\n\"It needs a place where people can stand without falling through the boards,\" Tamsin said.\n\n\"That can be special.\"\n\n\"Then start there.\"\n\nThe Tidewake festival was a small celebration marking the first safe passage of the spring tide. It had never been a grand event. Families made food, boat crews checked the moorings, and the children decorated the upper landing with paper fish.\n\nThis year, after the repairs and the Closed Bells inquiry, the children wanted a place to tell their own stories.\n\nNot a throne for Maui.\n\nA stage for everyone.\n\nLio, Fen's apprentice boatbuilder, had volunteered to help. They brought a sketch showing a low platform with a ramp, removable railings, and a storage space underneath for the paper decorations. It would be small enough to move when storms came.\n\nMaui examined the plan.\n\n\"I could build a bigger one! A stage that makes the sun jealous. We could carve my—\" He caught Lio's expression. \"All right, not my face. But something heroic. The stage would still be a masterpiece.\"\n\nLio looked at him.\n\n\"Why?\"\n\nHe opened his mouth.\n\nClosed it.\n\n\"Good question,\" he said.\n\nI bit back a smile.\n\nWe held a planning meeting beside the garden. The children wanted to perform little scenes about their favorite parts of the cove. One wanted to tell the story of the day the fish escaped the market. Another wanted to act out the time Fen fell into the water while testing a rope. Fen was not enthusiastic about that one.\n\nPip suggested the stage include a wide entrance so children carrying props didn't have to climb steps. Edda asked for a clear route around the audience seating. Tamsin set load limits. Danse offered to check the fasteners.\n\nMaui offered to lift the platform into place.\n\nThen he looked at Lio.\n\n\"Would you like help with that?\"\n\nLio grinned.\n\n\"Yes. But only when we've measured it.\"\n\nProgress.\n\nWe spent the next week building.\n\nThe children painted the panels, argued about which fish should be blue, and changed the program seventeen times. Maui helped carry the heavy boards but left the design decisions to the people who would use the stage. I helped write the schedule, then caught myself trying to make every child equally enthusiastic.\n\nSome wanted to perform.\n\nSome wanted to paint.\n\nSome wanted to watch.\n\nThat was enough.\n\nOn the morning of the festival, a storm rolled over the ridge.\n\nRain came in thin, sharp lines. The upper path grew slick. Fen checked the tide and declared that the lower landing would remain closed until the wind eased.\n\nMaui looked at the sky.\n\n\"I could blow the clouds away.\"\n\nAang, who had come to help with lanterns, raised an eyebrow.\n\n\"Not without moving the weather into somebody else's garden.\"\n\nMaui sighed.\n\n\"You're all ruining my dramatic instincts.\"\n\n\"That's the plan,\" I said.\n\nWe moved the festival into the sheltered hall by the square.\n\nThe stage had been built in sections, so the volunteers could carry it along the level route without risking the beach steps. Tamsin inspected each joint after assembly. The children rearranged their decorations around the room, delighted by the way the paper fish looked against the old stone.\n\nThe first performance began late.\n\nA little girl named Mira stood at the center of the stage and forgot her lines.\n\nThe audience waited.\n\nMaui, seated beside me, leaned forward.\n\nI felt him take in a breath large enough to power a whole speech. His hand shifted toward the edge of the bench, ready to stand and rescue Mira's forgotten line by telling the audience an even better story.\n\nMini Maui appeared against his ribs and pantomimed a tiny child trying to speak while a enormous man talked over her. The tattoo folded its arms.\n\n\"You have got to stop doing that,\" Maui whispered to his own chest.\n\n\"What?\" I whispered.\n\n\"Being right.\"\n\nMira was still looking at the cue card. Maui stayed seated, visibly trembling with the desire to help. He could lift an island out of the sea, but he couldn't hand a child the confidence to say her next line in her own time.\n\nWhen she finally spoke, he applauded louder than anyone. The child grinned. For a heartbeat he looked as proud as if he'd been the one onstage.\n\n\"That,\" he murmured to me, \"was pretty heroic.\"\n\n\"Her heroic moment.\"\n\n\"Obviously. I'm merely the world's finest audience.\"\n\nHe said it so grandly I laughed, and Mira's performance continued without him stealing the story.\n\nI touched his sleeve.\n\nHe stayed still.\n\nMira looked toward Pip, who held the cue cards.\n\nPip smiled and lifted the first line where she could see it.\n\nMira began again.\n\nThe room cheered when she finished.\n\nMaui looked at me.\n\n\"That was harder than fighting monsters.\"\n\n\"Letting her do it?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nI squeezed his hand.\n\nThe afternoon was full of mistakes. A paper fish fell off its string. Two children swapped roles in the middle of a scene and improvised an entirely new ending. Fen's fall into the water became a comedy involving a bucket and a very offended crab.\n\nFen laughed despite themself.\n\nMira found me beside the stage while the adults were packing the costumes. She held a torn paper fish against her chest.\n\n\"Did he really want to tell one of his stories?\" she asked.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Did I take his place?\"\n\nI crouched so she didn't have to look up at me.\n\n\"No. He decided he wanted to listen to yours.\"\n\nShe studied the fish, then glanced at Maui, who was struggling to fold a canvas screen without getting tangled in its ropes.\n\n\"He's not very good at that,\" she observed.\n\n\"He's learning.\"\n\nMira went over and asked if he needed help. He looked ready to say no, but after a moment he let her show him where the corner tied. She was proud of that in a way the applause hadn't quite managed.\n\nTamsin approached with the inspection board. The stage had passed its test, but one of the supports needed removing before the next tide. The children wanted to keep the platform for another day.\n\n\"We can ask them what they'd like to save,\" I suggested. \"The drawings can come down. The boards can be stored.\"\n\nTamsin nodded and asked the children. They chose to keep their paper fish and leave the stage pieces to the repair crew.\n\nMaui walked up carrying the folded screen.\n\n\"They didn't ask for a statue,\" he said, looking genuinely astonished.\n\n\"They asked for a place to tell a story.\"\n\n\"Which they got.\"\n\n\"And they'll need it again someday, perhaps. We can ask next time.\"\n\nHis hand found mine. He looked as though he wanted to say something grand, then leaned close enough to whisper.\n\n\"Do you think I did well?\"\n\n\"I think you listened when it was hard.\"\n\n\"That's almost praise.\"\n\n\"It is praise.\"\n\nHe kissed my forehead after checking my face for an answer. I smiled. The room around us was full of people taking their work home, children with their own stories and adults who would still need to inspect the stage in the morning. None of it existed solely so Maui could learn a lesson, and that made the celebration feel real.\n\nMaui never took the stage.\n\nAt supper, someone asked whether he would tell one of his famous stories.\n\nHe looked toward the children.\n\n\"They've got plenty of their own.\"\n\nThe person seemed surprised.\n\nThen they nodded.\n\nWe ate together while the rain softened outside.\n\nLater, when the hall was nearly empty, Maui found me by the little stage. He was carrying the spare hooks and a roll of tape, putting away the last of the decorations.\n\n\"Well?\" he asked.\n\n\"Well what?\"\n\n\"Did I behave?\"\n\n\"Mostly.\"\n\nHe grinned.\n\n\"High praise.\"\n\n\"You didn't rescue the play when Mira forgot her lines.\"\n\n\"Nearly did.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nHe looked at the floor.\n\n\"I wanted to be useful.\"\n\n\"You were. You helped build the platform. You carried it safely when the weather changed. You let the children own their own day.\"\n\nHe considered that.\n\n\"Does that count as a heroic deed?\"\n\n\"Only if you don't make a speech about it.\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\nI stepped closer.\n\n\"Can I tell you a secret?\"\n\n\"Absolutely.\"\n\n\"I liked watching you sit in the audience.\"\n\nHe looked surprised.\n\n\"Really?\"\n\n\"Yes. You looked happy when somebody else got the applause.\"\n\nHe took my hand.\n\n\"I was.\"\n\nWe stood beside the dark stage, the last lantern burning over the doorway.\n\n\"Do you want to go home?\" he asked.\n\n\"In a minute.\"\n\nHe leaned down and kissed me, warm and unhurried. I held the front of his coat and felt him smile against my mouth.\n\nWhen we pulled apart, he whispered, \"What are we waiting for?\"\n\n\"Nothing.\"\n\n\"Then why the minute?\"\n\n\"Because I like being here with you.\"\n\nHe went quiet.\n\nThen he rested his forehead against mine.\n\nWe stayed until the lantern keeper came to close the hall, at which point Maui offered to carry the hooks back to storage and got assigned a mop instead.\n\nHe used it without complaint.\n\nThe next morning, Mira left a small paper fish on our doorstep.\n\nOn the back she'd written: THANK YOU FOR LETTING ME TELL IT.\n\nMaui put it beside the wooden fish on the shelf.\n\nHe looked at both of them for a long time.\n\nI didn't ask what he was thinking.\n\nI think I knew.","word_count":1619},"maui-30":{"number":30,"title":"The Edge of the Storm","content":"The shore gate passed the first inspection and failed the second.\n\nDanse stared at the measurement as if the number had insulted him personally.\n\nToph stood beside the retaining wall with one hand resting against the stone.\n\n\"Told you,\" she said.\n\n\"You told me it was moving,\" Danse answered. \"You didn't tell me how much.\"\n\n\"I can't see your little ruler from here.\"\n\n\"You don't need to see it. You need to give me a repeatable observation.\"\n\nI looked at the tide chart.\n\nA storm was moving toward Tideglass. The old service passage beneath the cove remained sealed, but pressure readings at the lower gate had changed. The engineers had agreed to inspect the upper shore gate before the next heavy tide. It was the safest route for moving supplies away from the water, and the hinge had been repaired after the last flood.\n\nThe first measurement showed it opening freely.\n\nThe second, taken after the stone support had been dampened, showed the gate dragging against its frame.\n\nThe difference was small.\n\nIt was enough.\n\n\"Can we use it tonight?\" I asked.\n\nDanse looked at the figures.\n\n\"Not under full load.\"\n\nToph placed her palm against the support.\n\n\"The stone's shifting when the hinge takes weight. I can feel the pressure move through the wall.\"\n\nDanse shook his head.\n\n\"The displacement is within the margin for an unloaded gate.\"\n\n\"That's not the same as safe,\" Toph said.\n\nI felt the old temptation to pick whichever answer sounded more certain. Danse had calculations. Toph had experience that didn't fit neatly on the page. Both were trying to protect people.\n\nFen arrived with the updated tide prediction.\n\n\"The strongest surge will reach the lower landing after dark,\" they said. \"We need a route for the families by the cove.\"\n\n\"Then we close the gate and use the ridge path,\" I said.\n\nTamsin looked up from the hinge.\n\n\"The ridge is longer.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Some people will need assistance.\"\n\n\"Then we ask what they need and arrange it.\"\n\nDanse looked at me.\n\n\"You're making a decision before the test is complete.\"\n\n\"I'm deciding not to use an uncertain route during a storm. We can finish the test without putting people through it.\"\n\nHe considered that.\n\nThen he nodded.\n\n\"That's defensible.\"\n\nToph grinned.\n\n\"Look at that. The numbers and the dirt both agree.\"\n\nI almost laughed.\n\nThe work didn't end there.\n\nWe had to tell people the gate was closed, not merely hang a sign at a height half the village couldn't see. Pip wrote the notice. Edda checked the upper route for turning space and places to rest. Greg offered to carry lanterns but not to work past his agreed shift. Mara packed food for anyone who might need to wait at the hall.\n\nMaui watched the preparations with his hook on one shoulder. Each time Fen called out a new reading his fingers twitched, eager to fix something he could actually hit or lift. He was no less brave for standing there, but it was making him extremely bad-tempered. \"Just saying,\" he muttered, \"the storm and I could settle this in a much more entertaining way.\"\n\nA small child near the warm room pointed at his hook.\n\n\"Can you turn into a bird?\"\n\nMaui gave a grin that was practically a sunrise. \"Can I turn into a bird? Kid, I can turn into more birds than you've ever counted.\"\n\nHe shifted into a large hawk with a burst of feathers, tried to land on the doorway, and immediately discovered the lintel wasn't built for a bird that size. He landed on the floor with a disgruntled squawk.\n\nThe child laughed. Mini Maui appeared again when he changed back, pointing furiously at the low beam.\n\n\"Yeah, yeah. Terrible architecture.\"\n\nI checked the corridor was clear before he tried anything else. The child wanted him to do it again. He looked tempted; I could almost see his heroic introduction beginning.\n\n\"After the storm check,\" I said. \"If your parents and the staff agree.\"\n\nMaui sighed theatrically. \"Once again, the safety committee destroys the greatest show in history.\"\n\nHe carried the blankets into the warm room and made sure the child had somewhere to sit. His jokes were a way to keep the fear from filling the room. The fear was still there, and he knew it.\n\n\"I can carry people over the wall,\" he said.\n\n\"If somebody asks and it's safe,\" I answered.\n\nHe nodded.\n\n\"Right.\"\n\nThe storm arrived early.\n\nRain moved across the cove in sheets. The sea turned the color of iron. I helped Fen check the last marker by the signal post, then returned along the upper path as the wind grew strong enough to push at my coat.\n\nAt the hall, people were arriving in small groups. Some had chosen to leave their homes temporarily. Others stayed where the buildings were safe. We did not force anyone through a Threadway, and nobody had to explain why they preferred a particular place to wait.\n\nThe lanterns flickered.\n\nA child began to cry.\n\nMaui crouched beside her and asked whether she wanted a story. She nodded. He told her about a crab so vain it demanded a crown from the moon.\n\nShe laughed through her tears.\n\nThe lights went out halfway through the ending.\n\nFor a second the room was entirely dark.\n\nThen someone lit a hand lantern near the door. Another light appeared by the kitchen. Danse sent a message from Brasswatch: the generator had failed under the surge, but backup lamps were available and repairs were underway.\n\nWe counted people without making a public list of private destinations.\n\nEveryone in the hall was accounted for.\n\nThe hall went quiet after the bell. Fen kept their hand on the tide chart, waiting for the signal to end. The note faded slowly, like a cup humming after someone had touched the rim.\n\nA child asked whether the sound meant the lower gate was opening.\n\n\"No,\" Fen said. \"We don't have evidence of that. The old passage remains sealed. The pressure may be changing at a different point.\"\n\nThe answer was less exciting than a story about a door beneath the ocean. It also let everyone stay where they were instead of running toward a tunnel nobody had authorized them to enter.\n\nA volunteer brought in a fresh lantern and checked the path to the shelter entrance. The first sign had slipped in the wind. Edda pointed out that the replacement blocked the handrail when someone held it upright.\n\n\"Shift it left,\" she said. \"There. Now it gives directions without becoming another obstacle.\"\n\nWe moved two crates from the hallway. The pantry workers had already set aside food for the overnight watch, and the blankets Maui was carrying came from a labeled shelf that would need replenishing when the storm ended.\n\nI wanted to go outside and see what the waves were doing. I also knew that my wanting to witness danger didn't make me useful to the people around me.\n\n\"Could I help with the head count?\" I asked Neri.\n\n\"Yes. But use the private check-in sheet. We don't post people's destinations.\"\n\nMaui watched the process, blankets held carefully against his chest.\n\n\"All this work before anything happens,\" he said.\n\n\"That's how we give people choices if something does,\" Neri answered.\n\nHe looked at me over the last bundle. \"I suppose tearing the gate apart would be quicker.\"\n\n\"Quickly worse, maybe.\"\n\nHe laughed, then turned to help a visitor find the warm room. When he came back, he took my hand for one moment, not to claim my attention but to ask whether I was all right.\n\n\"I am,\" I said. \"I'm frightened, but I'm here.\"\n\n\"So am I.\"\n\nFor once that was the whole heroic speech. The next bell did not sound. We stayed until the watches were assigned and the lanterns were checked again.\n\nOutside, the second bell sounded.\n\nIt came from beneath the sea, deeper than the public bell, and held its note long enough to make the floor tremble. Fen looked toward the windows.\n\n\"Pressure change,\" they said.\n\n\"Can we do anything?\" I asked.\n\n\"Not safely from here.\"\n\nMaui stood.\n\nI saw the fight move across his face. His instinct was to run toward the sound and tear apart whatever held it.\n\nThen he looked at the children, the lanterns, and the people who had chosen to wait.\n\n\"What do you need me to do?\" he asked.\n\nFen handed him a stack of dry blankets.\n\n\"Take these to the west bench. Then stay where we can find you.\"\n\nHe did.\n\nThe storm passed after midnight. The lower landing was flooded, but the closed shore gate held. Its frame had shifted another fraction under the load, confirming the earlier test. Had we used it heavily, the hinge might have jammed.\n\nNo one had to discover that while standing in the doorway.\n\nAt dawn, Danse and Toph returned with Tamsin to examine the damage.\n\nDanse crouched beside the gate.\n\n\"The wet-load tolerance was inadequate,\" he said.\n\nToph tapped the stone.\n\n\"That's a polite way of saying it moved.\"\n\nHe looked at her.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nShe smiled.\n\n\"Good. Now let's fix it.\"\n\nI leaned against the railing, exhausted.\n\nMaui came up beside me with two cups of Iroh's house tea.\n\n\"How's the hero business?\" I asked.\n\n\"Terrible. I carried blankets.\"\n\n\"Very glamorous.\"\n\n\"I also told a crab story.\"\n\n\"That sounds like the important part.\"\n\nHe handed me a cup.\n\nWe stood in the cool morning air. The sea was still rough, and the ridge path needed clearing. There would be repairs, funding meetings, and another round of inspection before the gate reopened.\n\nMaui brushed his fingers against mine.\n\n\"Do you wish you'd let me break the lower passage open?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Neither do I.\"\n\nI looked at him.\n\nHe was watching the water, not the crowd.\n\n\"That surprises you?\" he asked.\n\n\"A little.\"\n\nHe smiled tiredly.\n\n\"I've been paying attention.\"\n\nI leaned into his shoulder.\n\nThe storm hadn't ended the mystery. It had made the evidence more urgent and the repairs more expensive.\n\nWe would have to decide what to do next with the people who lived here.\n\nFor now, the gate was closed because we had chosen safety over certainty.\n\nThat felt like the right kind of courage.","word_count":1716}}};for(const s of lib.series){const byId=edits[s.series_slug]||{};for(const c of s.chapters){const v=byId[c.id];if(!v||c.chapter_number!==v.number)continue;c.content=v.content;c.word_count=v.word_count;c.title=v.title;}}})();
